{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>Six of their names, etc.</b> One needs to know why it says <b>of their names</b> and does not say six names, etc., and six names upon the etc., according to their generations. Also, there was no need to say ״the remaining,״ for I see that they are remaining. And if one is forced to interpret <b>remaining</b> as meaning the lesser ones, in the manner of his saying ״concerning Elazar, etc., the remaining,״ and the Sages, z״l, interpreted (Yoma 87a; Zohar vol. III 62a) ״the lesser ones,״ etc., and as they also interpreted (Genesis Rabbah 83:9) regarding the verse ״the white sheep that remained״ — see there. And this is bewildering, for from his saying <b>according to their generations</b> it implies in the order they were born to Ya'akov, and if so why should those born last be lesser than the firstborn — all the more so since among the latter are sons of the matriarchs, Yosef and Binyamin, Zevulun and Yissaskhar a son of Torah, while among the former are sons of the maidservants — so how can the verse call the sons of the matriarchs ״remaining״ in comparison to the sons of the maidservants?" "And to understand the matter, I shall look to what they said in Sotah (36a), and this is their language: The Tanna'im taught: The Kohen Gadol had two stones on his shoulder, etc., and the names of the children of Israel were written upon them — six on this stone and six on that stone, as it says ״six,״ etc.; the second according to their generations and not the first according to their generations, because Yehudah is moved up. And fifty letters — twenty-five on this stone and twenty-five on that stone. Rabbi Chanina ben Gamli'el says: Not in the order they are divided in the book of Numbers, but in the order they are divided in the second book (Genesis). How so? The sons of Leah in their order, the sons of Rachel one from here and one from there, and the sons of the maidservants in the middle. But how do I establish ״according to their generations״? — the names that Ya'akov called them, and not the names that Moshe called them, ״Re'uveni, Shim'oni,״ etc., end quote. The plain sense of the matter is that for the first Tanna, ״according to their generations״ refers back to the names on the two stones — except that Yehudah was moved ahead of Re'uven in this manner: Yehudah, Re'uven, Shim'on, Levi, Dan, Naftali on one stone, and on the second stone: Gad, Asher, Yissaskhar, Zevulun, Yosef, and Binyamin, in the order of their birth. And when you count the letters you will find twenty-five on one and twenty-four on the other. And they said in the Talmud (Sotah 36a): Rabbi Yitzchak said, the letter hei was added to Yosef — Yehosef; Rav Nachman said, Binyamin is written full. And this calculation is not alluded to in the verse but rather the tradition came to them thus — twenty-five on one and twenty-five on the other. And the moving of Yehudah ahead of Re'uven, I say, is derived from the verse itself, from the fact that it wrote ״according to their generations״ regarding the latter (stone) and did not write in this manner ״and you shall engrave upon them the names of the children of Israel according to their generations״ — for then it would have been understood that the requirement of ״according to their generations״ applies to the order of both stones." "And one should not object: Who revealed that the reason the first stone is not ״according to their generations״ is because of Yehudah being moved ahead — perhaps one could say there is no order at all? This is not so, for necessarily, when you come to posit a different order on the first stone that is not ״according to their generations,״ you must choose one of the orders that exist in the verse — either the order written in the book of Numbers or in the second book (Genesis). And for each of them, when you come to arrange the first stone not according to their generations, the second stone will necessarily also come out not according to their generations. And if so, you have nullified ״according to their generations״ on both stones — so you are compelled to say that the change which nullifies ״according to their generations״ on the first stone is done in a manner that does not nullify it also on the second. And what does that look like? Necessarily in such a manner that Yehudah is moved ahead. And this matter is logical, for he is a king and it is fitting to give him precedence, and we also find that he went first in the dedication offerings of the altar. And although Yissaskhar also went first, if we were to give him precedence here we would nullify ״according to their generations״ of the second stone as well. And along this path, what we analyzed in its proper place is well-founded. And it is possible to say that the intention is to say that the order is not a disqualifying factor for the stones of the ephod — that if one moved Shim'on ahead of Re'uven it does not invalidate. Therefore it says <b>six of their names</b>, etc., <b>and the name of</b>, etc., <b>the remaining</b> — meaning, do not write except what remains in your hand that you did not write on the first one. And there is proof for this from the fact that the Tosefta of Menachot (Tosefta Menachot 5) did not teach that the order of writing the names disqualifies one against the other, as it taught: the priestly garments disqualify one against the other; the shoham stones and the milu'im stones disqualify one against the other; the inscription upon them disqualifies one against the other — it should have said as well: the order of the names disqualifies one against the other. It is possible that it holds it does not disqualify." "<b>And Rabbi</b> Chanina ben Gamli'el holds: On the first stone, the sons of Leah all in their order — meaning, one does not move up the sons of Zilpah even though they were born earlier. And on the second stone, Binyamin is at the top of the stone before the sons of the maidservants, as indeed written in the second book (Genesis), and after them the sons of the maidservants in the order of their birth, and Yosef at the bottom of the stone — and that is his saying ״one from here.״ And according to this order, the count of the letters does not come out divided twenty-five on this stone and twenty-five on that stone, for the first stone, on which are the sons of Leah in their order, has twenty-eight letters, and on the second stone twenty-one, and when you add the letter hei to Yosef or yod to Binyamin, they come to twenty-two. And according to this opinion, what we analyzed in the verse on this path is explained: <b>Six of their names</b> — meaning, not in their order: some who were born first and some who were born after the six — the remaining ones — namely, Yissaskhar and Zevulun, who were born after all the sons of the maidservants. And His saying <b>the remaining</b> — meaning, that became remaining from the order of the count: when you come to arrange the order of those born first, you will find that the two of them (the sons of the maidservants) preceded the two sons of Leah (Yissaskhar and Zevulun), and HaShem left them after them; therefore He called them ״remaining.״ And although Yosef and Binyamin are with them, it said ״the remaining״ on account of the four among them." "<b>And I shall come</b> to the matter of what the Rambam wrote in chapter 9 of Hilkhot Kelei HaMikdash, and this is his language: Six on this stone and six on that stone, and they write the name Yehosef, and it turns out twenty-five letters on this stone and twenty-five letters on that stone, and this is how they would write them, end quote. And his words, may his memory be for a blessing, are neither according to the first Tanna nor according to Rabbi Chanina ben Gamli'el (Sotah 36a) — this is nothing but a new construction, and not according to the opinion of Rav Kahana which the Talmud cited and rejected, for he said on the first stone: Re'uven, Gad, Asher, Zevulun, Dan, Naftali, and on the second stone Shim'on, Levi, Yehudah, Yissaskhar, Yosef, Binyamin, etc. And the Maharik (in Kesef Mishneh) pressed and explained that the Rambam ruled according to Rabbi Chanina ben Gamli'el, and he explains the words of Rabbi Chanina ben Gamli'el in this manner: the sons of Leah in their order, meaning one from here and one from here; the sons of Rachel one from here and one from there at the two ends of the stones below; and the sons of the maidservants in the middle, meaning in the middle of the two stones, end quote." "<b>But to me</b> it appears that these matters are not acceptable to the heart of every wise-hearted person, for four reasons. First: how could the Tanna Kamma set aside his own position and rule like Rabbi Chanina? And what the Rav said — that the reasoning of Rabbi Chanina ben Gamliel is more plausible — on the contrary, the reasoning of Rabbi Chanina ben Gamliel is not plausible at all, for it removes the verse from its plain meaning, since he explained ״כְּתוֹלְדֹתָם״ as — like the names that their father called them and not like the names that Moses called them; for whence would it arise upon the mind of man that he would write רְאוּבֵנִי and גָדִי, which would require abbreviating them? Furthermore, ״כְּתוֹלְדֹתָם״ does not well imply what he wants to say, namely their names at birth, and there is no necessity to remove the word from its plain sense, which is according to the order of their birth, as is the reasoning of the Tanna Kamma, and the reasoning of the Tanna Kamma is far more plausible. And this difficulty is easy compared to what follows it." "Second: according to the Rav's reasoning, who said that the sons of Leah — one from this side, like the sons of Rachel — why did Rabbi Chanina ben Gamliel say: ״the sons of Leah in their order, the sons of Rachel — one from this side and one from that side,״ which indicates with a finger that the sons of Leah are not ״one from this side and one from that side״? For if the meaning of ״in their order״ is one from this side and one from that side, then the sons of Rachel too follow the order of the sons of Leah, and he should have said nothing other than ״the sons of Rachel at the end.״ Furthermore, the meaning of ״one from this side and one from that side״ would not express his words, for he should have said ״one on this stone and one on that stone,״ since ״one from this side״ etc. expresses the view of Rashi, namely one at the head of the stone and one at its end. And this difficulty is easy compared to what follows it." "Third: according to the explanation of the Rav's words, this order does not exist in the second book of the Torah, for in the second book the sons of Leah are one after another and Binyamin after them, and the sons of the maidservants after Binyamin, and Yosef at the end. And according to Rabbi Chanina ben Gamliel as per the Rambam, Yosep is after the two sons of the maidservants on the first stone, and Binyamin later, after the two latter sons of the maidservants on the second stone — and how could Rabbi Chanina ben Gamliel say ״as they are divided in the second book״? For in the second book Binyamin precedes all the sons of the maidservants! And further, why did he put Yosef before Binyamin, when in the second book Binyamin precedes? And one cannot push this aside by saying he reversed them in order to work out the count of twenty-five on each stone, since their count is equal — five on each one. And in truth, if Rabbi Chanina ben Gamliel were to stand and say that this is what he intended, his words would not hold up, since he made the matter dependent and said ״as they are divided in the second book,״ and also his words are not acceptable, for the verse put Binyamin before the sons of the maidservants, while according to him he comes in the very last position." "Fourth: whence did the Rambam find to arrange the sons of Leah — half on this stone and half on that stone? For the plain sense of the words that Rabbi Chanina ben Gamliel said, ״the sons of Leah in their order,״ does not imply thus, and whence did this reasoning arise for the Rambam from the words of Rabbi Chanina ben Gamliel? And the latter authorities were troubled by what the Kesef Mishneh wrote in explanation of the Rambam, who wrote to complete the fifty letters by adding a ה׳ to Yosep, and he raised an objection to this from the Talmud, which says there (Sotah 36a), and this is its language: ״these fifty — fifty lacking one they are. Rabbi Yitzchak said: Yosep — they added one letter for him, as it is said (Psalms 81:6) ״עֵדוּת בִּיהוֹסֵף״. Rav Nachman objects: ״כְּתוֹלְדֹתָם״ they need to be! Rather, throughout the Torah [it is] בִּנְיָמִין [with two yods] — but here בִּנְיָמִין is complete, as it is written (Genesis 35:18) ״וְאָבִיו קָרָא לוֹ בִּנְיָמִין״ — end quote. Behold, the Talmud rejected the Yehosep solution, for Yehosep does not hold up, and raised instead that בִּנְיָמִין is written with two yods. And the Rav answered that the Talmud's question from ״כְּתוֹלְדֹתָם״ applies only according to the Tanna Kamma, but according to the view of Rabbi Chanina ben Gamliel he does not ask, for he did not say to remove ״כְּתוֹלְדֹתָם״ but only that it should not write Re'uveni Shim'oni, and since this is so, the first resolution of Yehosep remains in its place, and our Master (the Rav) ruled like Rabbi Chanina ben Gamliel, as cited — end." "<b>And his words</b> are extremely wondrous in my eyes. For according to the Tanna Kamma, he did not examine the names at all, and he interprets the word ״כְּתוֹלְדֹתָם״ as referring to the order of their birth, one by one — so what place is there for the Talmud to ask, according to the Tanna Kamma, from ״כְּתוֹלְדֹתָם״? Rather, it is certain that he did not ask in the first place except according to the view of Rabbi Chanina ben Gamliel, who interprets ״כְּתוֹלְדֹתָם״ as the names that their father called them, and Rav Nachman resolved it with בִּנְיָמִין [written] full. And if we grant the assumption that the question applies according to the Tanna Kamma, then all the more so and by a fortiori it should apply according to the reasoning of Rabbi Chanina ben Gamliel, who examines ״כְּתוֹלְדֹתָם״ as referring to the names. Furthermore, from the words of the Rambam it is evident that he interprets ״כְּתוֹלְדֹתָם״ as referring to the order and not to the names, for he wrote, and this is his language: ״six on this stone and six on that stone, as their generations,״ end quote. And if the Rambam's intention had been to rule like Rabbi Chanina ben Gamliel, he would not have gone so far as to say ״כְּתוֹלְדֹתָם,״ for we have well seen that the Talmud asked about it and needed to explain it — rather it is certain that he did not rule like Rabbi Chanina ben Gamliel. Therefore it would have been more correct to leave the matter as requiring further study than to say such a thing." "<b>And it is possible</b> to say that Rambam did not have in his text the words of the first Tanna, because Judah is first, but rather in this manner: the second row <b>according to their birth</b> — meaning, in the arrangement of the second row they are found ordered according to their birth in this way: Reuben on one, Simeon on the second, Levi on one, Judah on the second, and so on in this manner. It turns out that when you come to read them in order, the two rows will be according to their birth. And this is what it says, the second row is according to their birth, but the first row is not according to their birth, for they are arranged in it in this way: Reuben, Levi, Issachar — and this is not according to their birth in any manner. And the meaning of <b>according to their birth</b> before us is two ways: the first is the order of the book of Numbers, which is in this manner — the sons of Leah together, the sons of Rachel after them, the sons of the handmaids after them. And the second is as stated in the second book [Genesis] — the sons of Leah first, the sons of the handmaids last, the sons of Rachel — Joseph at the very end and Benjamin between the sons of Leah and the sons of the handmaids. And the first Tanna did not specify which he chose, and even though he said ״according to their birth״ there is no proof for his opinion, for it is possible to say ״according to their birth״ even if he precedes all the sons of Leah on the grounds that he does not interrupt between the sons of one mother, and even though a minority of them were born after the sons of Bilhah — it is possible to say ״according to their birth״ in the order of the mothers: Leah gave birth first and all her sons are reckoned as one to precede them all together, or also on the grounds that the majority of the sons came first. And also because we do not find that they were arranged in the entire Torah in the order of their birth in this manner. And Rambam analyzed precisely the words of Rabbi Hanina ben Gamliel, who said: not in the order that they are divided in the book of Numbers, divided, etc. — this indicates that this matter can enter the words of the first Tanna, who comes to negate it. And this is your sign: for the intent of the first Tanna in saying ״according to their birth״ is in one of the two ways — either in the order of the book of Numbers or the order of the second book, and Rabbi Hanina ben Gamliel came and negated the book of Numbers and chose the second book. And Rambam held that there is no necessity in the words of the first Tanna, and it is possible that such is the opinion of the first Tanna to explain it according to the order of the book of Numbers. And even though from the words of Rabbi Hanina ben Gamliel it appears that the first Tanna holds thus, since his words are balanced [equivocal], therefore he needed to explain; but we have no right to explain the words of the first Tanna except according to the opinion of Rabbi Hanina ben Gamliel, as long as it is not found explicitly that he disagreed with him." "And the dispute between Rabbi Hanina ben Gamliel and the first Tanna concerns the meaning of <b>according to their birth.</b> The first Tanna holds that the second row is according to their birth in the manner we explained, but Rabbi Hanina ben Gamliel said: the sons of Leah in their order — meaning, on one stone the sons of Rachel, one at the head of the stone and one at the end, and the sons of the handmaids in the middle of the stone; if so, they are not according to their birth, and it was necessary to explain ״according to their names״ etc. And the Rav [Rambam] ruled according to the first Tanna and arranged the sons of Leah — one on this stone and one on that stone — so that the second row would come out according to their birth, and the sons of the handmaids after them in the order mentioned. And the reason he preceded them is that they were arranged in the second book before Joseph, and in this too the statement ״according to their birth״ is justified, for if he were to precede Joseph and Benjamin, then even the second row would not be according to their birth, since the sons of Rachel would precede the sons of the handmaids. And the reason he preceded Naphtali to Dan was to complete twenty-five letters, as the first Tanna said, for it was not Rabbi Hanina ben Gamliel who said this; and even though there is room to say that from the implication he does not disagree, from the thrust of the matter one is compelled to say that he does disagree, since he divided them into an order that yields twenty-eight on one and twenty-two on the other. And afterward he arranged Joseph and Benjamin and completed the count with the letter ב in ״יהוסף״ (biYosef) — for the Talmud did not reject this reasoning except according to Rabbi Hanina ben Gamliel, but not according to the first Tanna, for he does not interpret ״according to their birth״ as referring to the names. And he did not write Judah at the beginning because he did not have it in his text of the Talmud, as we have written. This is what appears forced to us in order to settle the baraita according to Rambam's reasoning. Or perhaps some baraita came to his hand and he ruled accordingly." "And be that as it may, according to this approach of Rambam the fine points that we examined in the verse will be settled correctly. Its saying <b>six of their names</b> and not saying ״six names,״ for then you would understand that they are in their order according to their birth on each and every stone; rather, <b>of their names</b> means: from every two of them take one and write it on the one stone, and one will remain; and so for the second pair and the third pair, etc. And afterward take those six that remained from each and every pair and they will go on the second stone — and on this basis he was precise in saying <b>and the names of the six that remain</b> — meaning, that remain in your hand as stated above. And accordingly, its saying <b>according to their birth</b> refers to their order across the two stones, and therefore he delayed saying <b>according to their birth</b> until after he mentioned the two stones. And understand:"], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 28, :notes {4 "The reference to Maharik appears to mean the Kesef Mishneh commentary of R. Vidal di Tolosa on Rambam, though Maharik (R. Joseph Colon) is not its author; this may reflect a variant attribution or Or HaChaim's shorthand."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:35:33.444620007Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}